(>> Solution to Question 9:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the grammatical errors of dangling and misplaced participles in biotechnology papers, providing clear correction methods.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Syntactic Nature of the Dangling Participle:) Tj
T*
(   A participial modifier must have a clearly stated grammatical subject immediately adjacent to) Tj
T*
(   it that logically performs the action denoted by the participle. When the logical agent is) Tj
T*
(   absent or placed elsewhere, the participle 'dangles'.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Problematic Scientific Example:) Tj
T*
(   - Dangling: "Centrifuging the bacterial lysate at 10,000 g, the pellet was observed.") Tj
T*
(   - Analysis: Syntactically, 'the pellet' is the subject of the main clause, falsely implying) Tj
T*
(   that the inanimate pellet performed the centrifugation.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Methods of Grammatical Correction:) Tj
T*
(   - Method A \(Supply the human agent in the main clause\):) Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "Centrifuging the bacterial lysate at 10,000 g, the investigators observed a white) Tj
T*
(   pellet.") Tj
T*
(   - Method B \(Convert the participial phrase into an adverbial dependent clause\):) Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "When the bacterial lysate was centrifuged at 10,000 g, a distinct pellet formed at) Tj
T*
(   the tube bottom.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 10:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze transitive, intransitive, and ergative verbs in experimental biology procedures.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Transitive Verbs:) Tj
T*
(   Require one or more direct objects to complete their syntactic meaning. In active voice, the) Tj
T*
(   direct object receives the action:) Tj
T*
(   - Example: "The restriction enzyme cleaved the circular vector." \(Cleaved + object 'the) Tj
T*
(   circular vector'\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Intransitive Verbs:) Tj
T*
(   Do not take a direct object; the action terminates within the subject:) Tj
T*
(   - Example: "The cell culture deteriorated rapidly under thermal stress." \(Deteriorated has no) Tj
T*
(   object\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Ergative \(Labile\) Verbs in Scientific English:) Tj
T*
(   Verbs that can function both transitively and intransitively, where the direct object of the) Tj
T*
(   transitive form becomes the subject of the intransitive form:) Tj
T*
(   - Transitive: "The researcher lysed the bacterial cells.") Tj
T*
(   - Intransitive: "The bacterial cells lysed after lysozyme treatment.") Tj
T*
(   - Transitive: "We precipitated the nucleic acids with ice-cold ethanol.") Tj
T*
(   - Intransitive: "The nucleic acids precipitated after addition of ethanol.") Tj
T*
(   - Examples of Ergative Verbs: dissolve, incubate, boil, freeze, rupture, open, aggregate, melt,) Tj
T*
(   change.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

