(   When two or more words combine to modify a following noun as a single unified concept, they are) Tj
T*
(   hyphenated to prevent syntactic ambiguity:) Tj
T*
(   - Examples: "high-throughput screening", "double-stranded DNA", "temperature-sensitive mutant",) Tj
T*
(   "membrane-bound receptor", "site-directed mutagenesis".) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Post-Noun Predicative Exception:) Tj
T*
(   When the compound adjective follows the noun \(in predicate position\), the hyphen is generally) Tj
T*
(   omitted:) Tj
T*
(   - Pre-nominal: "We utilized a state-of-the-art flow cytometer.") Tj
T*
(   - Post-nominal: "The flow cytometer was state of the art.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Adverb-Adjective Combinations Ending in -ly:) Tj
T*
(   Adverbs ending in -ly never take a hyphen when preceding an adjective, because the -ly suffix) Tj
T*
(   already clearly signals an adverbial modifier:) Tj
T*
(   - Incorrect: "a highly-conserved sequence", "a chemically-defined medium") Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "a highly conserved sequence", "a chemically defined medium") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
