(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze coordinating conjunctions \(FANBOYS\) and the rules for compound sentences versus compound predicates.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Coordinating Conjunction Inventory:) Tj
T*
(   The seven coordinating conjunctions are For, And, Nor, But, Or, Yet, So \(FANBOYS\). They connect) Tj
T*
(   grammatically equivalent syntactic elements \(words, phrases, independent clauses\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Punctuation with Independent Clauses:) Tj
T*
(   When connecting two independent clauses \(each having its own subject and finite verb\), a comma) Tj
T*
(   must precede the coordinating conjunction:) Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "The recombinant cells synthesized the therapeutic protein, and the downstream) Tj
T*
(   purification process yielded high purity.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Punctuation with Compound Predicates:) Tj
T*
(   When two verbs share the same subject without a second explicit subject pronoun, no comma) Tj
T*
(   should separate the verbs:) Tj
T*
(   - Incorrect: "The technician centrifuged the sample, and decanted the clear supernatant.") Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "The technician centrifuged the sample and decanted the clear supernatant.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 19:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Discuss correlative conjunctions and the imperative of syntactic parallelism in academic biotechnology writing.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Correlative Conjunction Pairs:) Tj
T*
(   - Either...or; Neither...nor; Both...and; Not only...but also; Whether...or.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Principle of Strict Syntactic Parallelism:) Tj
T*
(   The grammatical element immediately following the first correlative marker must match the) Tj
T*
(   grammatical category following the second marker:) Tj
T*
(   - Faulty: "The protocol not only required high temperatures [verb phrase] but also extensive) Tj
T*
(   cooling periods [noun phrase].") Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "The protocol required not only high temperatures [noun phrase] but also extensive) Tj
T*
(   cooling periods [noun phrase].") Tj
T*
(   - Faulty: "The enzyme was evaluated both by gel electrophoresis and using mass spectrometry.") Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "The enzyme was evaluated both by gel electrophoresis and by mass spectrometry.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 20:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine subordinating conjunctions of cause, condition, and concession in experimental results reporting.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Subordinating Conjunction Functions:) Tj
T*
(   Subordinating conjunctions introduce dependent adverbial clauses, establishing nuanced) Tj
T*
(   cognitive links with the independent clause:) Tj
T*
(   - Cause and Reason: because, since, as. In formal scientific English, 'because' is preferred) Tj
T*
(   for direct physical causality, while 'since' and 'as' are acceptable when the cause is already) Tj
T*
(   known to the reader.) Tj
T*
(   - Concession and Contrast: although, even though, whereas, while.) Tj
T*
(   - 'Whereas' establishes direct contrast between parallel phenomena: "Gram-positive bacteria) Tj
T*
(   retain crystal violet, whereas Gram-negative cells do not.") Tj
T*
(   - 'While' should be reserved for temporal simultaneity, though widely accepted concessively.) Tj
T*
ET

