(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the grammatical status and capitalization rules for scientific binomial nomenclature and proper nouns.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Morphological Nature of Binomial Nomenclature:) Tj
T*
(   The scientific name of an organism is a Latinized binomial proper noun composed of the Genus) Tj
T*
(   name followed by the specific epithet:) Tj
T*
(   - Genus: Capitalized proper noun \(e.g., Escherichia, Saccharomyces, Homo\).) Tj
T*
(   - Specific Epithet: Lowercase adjective or genitive noun \(e.g., coli, cerevisiae, sapiens\).) Tj
T*
(   - Typography: Must be italicized in print or underlined in handwritten text.) Tj
T*
(   - Abbreviation: After the first full mention \("Escherichia coli"\), the genus may be abbreviated) Tj
T*
(   to its initial capital letter followed by a period: "E. coli".) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Derivative Common Nouns:) Tj
T*
(   When taxonomic names are anglicized or used in a general sense, they lose capitalization and) Tj
T*
(   italicization:) Tj
T*
(   - "Bacillus subtilis" \(Proper taxonomic binomial\) vs. "a rod-shaped bacillus" \(Common) Tj
T*
(   morphological noun\).) Tj
T*
(   - "Streptococcus pneumoniae" vs. "streptococcal infections".) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
